.page tail 1 write the last part of files

# SYNOPSIS
*tail* [*-qvfF*] [*-n* _N_ | *-n* *+*_N_ | *-c* _N_ | *-*_N_] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`tail` writes the last ten lines of each _FILE_ (standard input if there is none, or for `-`). With several files each is introduced by a `==> NAME <==` line.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n* _N_
    Write the last _N_ lines.
@ *-n* *+*_N_
    Start at line _N_ and write everything from there to the end.
@ *-c* _N_
    Count bytes instead of lines; `-c +`_N_ starts at byte _N_.
@ *-*_N_
    A shorthand for `-n` _N_.
@ *-q*, *-v*
    Never / always write the `==> NAME <==` headers.
@ *-f*, *-F*
    After writing the end of the last file, keep it open and write what is appended to it, checking five times a second. `tail` never stops by itself in this mode; interrupt it with ^C.

Counts take the same suffixes as in head(1).

# EXAMPLES
    tail -n 5 /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    tail -n +2 table.txt
    dmesg | tail -20
    tail -f build.log

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 for a bad option or count.

# SEE ALSO
head(1), cat(1), dmesg(1)
